(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-dermatology-medications-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology Medications Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 1","category":"Pharmacology I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which set gives the MAJOR indicators of atopic dermatitis as the lecture lists them?","choices":["Nikolsky sign, mucosal erosion at two or more sites, and fever preceding the rash by one to three days","Comedones, papulopustular lesions, and nodules","Pruritus, rash in areas typical of the disease, chronic or repeated symptoms, and family history","Raised immunoglobulin E, positive allergy skin tests, and dry patchy scales"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Raised immunoglobulin E, positive skin tests, dry patchy scales and infection susceptibility are the minor ones. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say to apply topical acne therapy to the whole affected area rather than to individual spots?","choices":["Spot application increases the risk of systemic absorption","The vehicle is only stable when spread thinly over a wide area","Individual lesions are already resolving by the time they are visible","Topical therapy only works where it is applied"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and that is why the whole affected area is treated rather than visible lesions alone. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does the lecture divide acne lesions?","choices":["Noninflammatory as open and closed comedones; inflammatory as papulopustular and nodular lesions","Noninflammatory as papules; inflammatory as comedones","Superficial as pustular; deep as comedonal","Acute disease as nodular and pustular lesions; chronic disease as open and closed comedones alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. That split is what decides whether topical or systemic therapy is chosen. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What is the mechanism the lecture gives for the topical azole antifungals?","choices":["They inhibit ergosterol production directly","They inhibit uptake of molecular precursors at the fungal cell wall","They bind fungal transfer RNA to stop protein synthesis","They inhibit fungal cytochrome P450, preventing cell wall formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the named agents are clotrimazole, ketoconazole, miconazole and sertaconazole. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which patient should not be given a topical retinoid, per the lecture?","choices":["A patient taking an oral contraceptive","A patient over sixty years of age","A patient who is pregnant","A patient with a history of eczema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture says to avoid retinoids during pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the depot effect described in the lecture, and what does it allow?","choices":["Repeated applications saturate hair follicles and shut down absorption","Skin acts as a reservoir for the drug, which may permit once daily dosing of short acting drugs","The drug is stored in subcutaneous fat and released during exercise, which is why activity increases absorption","The vehicle evaporates and leaves crystals that dissolve slowly in sweat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The reservoir is what lets a short acting drug be given less often than its half-life suggests. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why does the lecture include a sedating antihistamine in the non-pharmacologic advice for a child with atopic dermatitis?","choices":["To reverse the skin barrier dysfunction","To treat the secondary skin infections","To lower serum immunoglobulin E","To reduce night-time itching"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The sedating property is being used for the itch that disrupts sleep. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which agents does the lecture call the gold standard for atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Sedating antihistamines","Topical corticosteroids","Topical immunomodulators","Oral corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with the agent chosen by severity and by site of disease. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which use does the lecture give for mupirocin beyond ordinary skin infection?","choices":["Eliminating nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Eliminating vulvovaginal candidiasis in women","Treating recurrent orolabial herpes simplex","Treating onychomycosis of the toenails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and its coverage is most gram positive aerobes, especially meticillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic does the lecture name as the preferred agent for acne, and what is happening to the alternative?","choices":["Mupirocin is the preferred agent for acne; bacitracin is losing efficacy to Propionibacterium acnes resistance","Neomycin is preferred; polymyxin B is losing efficacy","Clindamycin is preferred; erythromycin is losing efficacy to Propionibacterium acnes resistance","Erythromycin is preferred; clindamycin is losing efficacy to resistance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and both are noted to lack systemic side effects. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"The lecture says occlusion increases efficacy. What does it flag alongside that?","choices":["It reliably shortens the required duration of therapy","It prevents the vehicle from exerting any therapeutic effect of its own","It makes regional variation in penetration irrelevant","It raises the question of increased toxicity as well"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs the efficacy gain with a query about toxicity rather than presenting it as free benefit. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient develops pustular inflammation on the trunk a few weeks after starting a systemic corticosteroid. What does the lecture say happens when the steroid is stopped?","choices":["No change, because the eruption is independent of the drug","An initial worsening, because inflammation increases","Immediate and complete resolution of the eruption","Conversion of the pustules into open comedones"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and warning the patient of this in advance is what stops them abandoning the plan. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which systemic effects does the lecture attribute to topical corticosteroids?","choices":["Raised serum lipids and brittle nails","Photosensitivity, severe sunburn, desquamation, burning and stinging that decrease with continued use","Adrenal suppression, infections, hyperglycaemia, glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children","Hepatotoxicity, nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That a topical agent can do all of this is the point of the potency and duration warnings. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which organism does the lecture name as proliferating in the anaerobic conditions created by pooled sebum?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Malassezia furfur","Streptococcus pyogenes","Propionibacterium acnes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pooled sebum creates anaerobic conditions in which this organism proliferates. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of benzoyl peroxide most needs to be raised with a patient before they start it?","choices":["It stains the skin orange for the duration of therapy","It can bleach hair, clothing and bedding","It causes a permanent blue-grey discolouration of the skin","It commonly causes systemic hypersensitivity reactions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is the practical warning that prevents a patient ruining clothes and abandoning the drug. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How often is imiquimod applied, per the lecture?","choices":["Two to five times per week","Twice daily without interruption","Once monthly","Only at night, because it is photolabile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is a weekly schedule rather than a daily one. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which two groups are contraindicated from receiving tetracyclines, per the lecture?","choices":["Children under eight years and pregnant women","Adolescents and the elderly","Patients with eczema and patients with psoriasis","Patients taking oral contraceptives and patients on spironolactone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These are the two the lecture names. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which pigmentary adverse effect does the lecture attach to azelaic acid?","choices":["Hypopigmentation","Hyperpigmentation of sun-exposed areas","Slate-grey discolouration of the nail beds","Yellow staining of the sclera"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is listed with mild skin irritation and dryness. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which antiandrogen does the lecture name for acne?","choices":["Ketoconazole","Cyproterone","Spironolactone","Finasteride"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and the lecture notes a gel formulation is available alongside oral use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What happens to benzoyl peroxide after it penetrates the stratum corneum?","choices":["It remains unchanged in the skin and works purely by oxidising sebum within the follicle","It is converted to benzoic acid, which is active against Propionibacterium acnes","It is converted to salicylic acid, which is keratinolytic","It is converted to retinoic acid, which corrects keratinization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It crosses unchanged and is converted once through. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A patient is starting a topical azole. What does the lecture support telling them about duration?","choices":["Treatment must continue for at least six months","Treatment is generally prolonged, on the order of two to three weeks","A single application is usually curative","Treatment continues until the rash disappears, then stops immediately"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and stopping early is how a treated infection returns. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which of these is a LOCAL adverse effect of topical steroids in the lecture, rather than a systemic one?","choices":["Adrenal suppression","Cataracts","Growth retardation in children","Skin atrophy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Local effects are skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis related to the vehicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A lesion is oozing, vesicular and crusting. Which end of the vehicle range does the lecture pair with that presentation?","choices":["The middle of the range, regardless of the lesion","Vehicle choice is not linked to lesion morphology","The drying end of the range","The least drying end of the range"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Oozing, vesiculation and crusting are matched to drying vehicles; scaling, lichenification and xerosis to the least drying. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Most topical acne agents cause skin irritation. What does the lecture say that leads to, and how does it advise starting?","choices":["It confirms the drug is working, so maintain the starting strength","It requires an immediate switch to systemic therapy, because irritation means the topical agent has failed","It is prevented entirely by using an alcoholic solution","It can lead to patient discontinuation, so start with lower strength and increase gradually"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anticipating the irritation is what keeps a patient on therapy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why does the lecture say tretinoin should be applied at night?","choices":["It is photolabile","Sebum production peaks overnight","It stains clothing if applied during the day","It must be applied at the same time as benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Being degraded by light is the reason for nightly application, distinct from the photosensitivity it also causes. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the anti-inflammatory action the lecture attributes to tetracyclines in acne?","choices":["They stimulate macrophages to release tumour necrosis factor alpha","They chelate calcium ions, which prevents neutrophil and monocyte chemotaxis","They stabilise mast cell membranes and block histamine release","They inhibit the conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is an anti-inflammatory action separate from killing the organism. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which agent does the lecture place in class 1, superpotent?","choices":["Triamcinolone acetonide cream","Clobetasol propionate","Hydrocortisone","Desonide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, listed in class 1 in cream, ointment, lotion, spray, shampoo and foam forms. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What are acyclovir and penciclovir used for topically in the lecture, and what kind of molecule are they?","choices":["Recurrent orolabial herpes simplex infection; they are synthetic guanine analogues","External and perianal warts; they are immunomodulators","Vulvovaginal candidiasis; they are azoles","Actinic keratoses and superficial basal cell carcinoma; they are topical retinoid preparations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, active against herpes simplex virus types 1 and 2. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which gap in coverage does the lecture flag for tolnaftate?","choices":["It has no activity below the stratum corneum","It has no activity on the scalp","It has no Candida activity","It has no dermatophyte activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and nystatin is the agent the lecture pairs with candidal infection. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What does imiquimod do, per the lecture?","choices":["It stimulates peripheral mononuclear cells to release interferon alpha and macrophages to produce tumour necrosis factor alpha and interleukins","It inhibits T cell, mast cell and keratinocyte activation","It is converted to benzoic acid after crossing the stratum corneum","It corrects abnormal follicular keratinization, reduces bacterial counts within the follicle, and dampens the inflammatory response"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is an immunomodulator rather than a directly cytotoxic agent. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"}]};
